At 3000°K, the equilibrium composition of a mixture of carbon monoxide, carbon dioxide, and molecular oxygen can be determined using the Gibbs free energy equation:

G = ΔH - TΔS

where G is the Gibbs free energy, ΔH is the enthalpy change, T is the temperature, and ΔS is the entropy change.

We can calculate the enthalpy changes and entropy changes for the three species as follows:

ΔH_CO = -284 kJ/mol
ΔH_CO2 = -393 kJ/mol
ΔH_O2 = -297 kJ/mol

ΔS_CO = 184 J/mol·K
ΔS_CO2 = 224 J/mol·K
ΔS_O2 = 244 J/mol·K

Now, we can calculate the Gibbs free energy of the three species at 3000°K:

G_CO = ΔH_CO - TΔS_CO = -284 kJ/mol - (3000 K)(184 J/mol·K) = -284 kJ/mol - 55200 J/mol = -5804 kJ/mol

G_CO2 = ΔH_CO2 - TΔS_CO2 = -393 kJ/mol - (3000 K)(224 J/mol·K) = -393 kJ/mol - 67280 J/mol = -7121 kJ/mol

G_O2 = ΔH_O2 - TΔS_O2 = -297 kJ/mol - (3000 K)(244 J/mol·K) = -297 kJ/mol - 73680 J/mol = -7665 kJ/mol

Now, we can calculate the equilibrium composition of the mixture using the Gibbs free energy equation:

x_CO = G_CO / (G_CO + G_CO2 + G_O2)

x_CO2 = G_CO2 / (G_CO + G_CO2 + G_O2)

x_O2 = G_O2 / (G_CO + G_CO2 + G_O2)

Substituting the values, we get:

x_CO = -5804 kJ/mol / (-5804 kJ/mol - 7121 kJ/mol - 7665 kJ/mol) = 0.099

x_CO2 = -7121 kJ/mol / (-5804 kJ/mol - 7121 kJ/mol - 7665 kJ/mol) = 0.60

x_O2 = 7665 kJ/mol / (-5804 kJ/mol - 7121 kJ/mol - 7665 kJ/mol) = 0.30

Therefore, the equilibrium composition of the mixture is:

x_CO = 0.099
x_CO2 = 0.60
x_O2 = 0.30

Option A is the correct answer.
